<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings.BehaviorCard">
  <!-- Six Windows 11 Settings-style rows: an icon, a title + secondary line, and the control trailing —
       the row spec's base variant, six times (see the "row spec's shared classes" block in
       Themes/Fluent2/Launcher.axaml for the anatomy and for what each class carries, and
       AppearanceCard's code-behind for the full ReloadFromConfig contract every settings card group
       follows).

       Round 5, item 3 rebuilt two of these six. 固定到任务栏 and 热键 used to wrap their Grid in a
       StackPanel and hang their status/help line BELOW it, indented by a hand-written Margin="30,8,0,0",
       on the argument that their control "needs more room than the trailing column offers". That
       argument does not survive measurement: both controls leave over 350 DIP of text column even at the
       app's documented minimum window width. What the arrangement did produce was a 14.00 and a 14.67
       DIP hole between each row's title and its own text — the below-row margin plus the slack under a
       bare, vertically centred title in a grid row sized by a 32 DIP control — and an icon sitting at a
       different y from every other row on the page. Both lines are now the header's own subtitle, which
       is where the spec puts a line that describes the row rather than a value the row holds, and both
       cards are ordinary two-line rows.

       OnContent/OffContent are no longer blanked per row: ToggleSwitch.tm-card-toggle does it for all of
       them, and carries the negative right margin that puts the pill's ink on the same x as every other
       row's control. Avalonia's default content is the untranslated English "On"/"Off", which would sit
       next to a Chinese row label whenever AppLanguage is zh-Hans. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock x:Name="GroupHead" Classes="tm-group-head" />

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xED1A;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="AutoCloseTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="AutoCloseSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <ToggleSwitch Grid.Column="2" x:Name="AutoCloseToggle" Classes="tm-card-toggle"
                      IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoCloseChanged" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE718;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="TopMostTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="TopMostSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <ToggleSwitch Grid.Column="2" x:Name="TopMostToggle" Classes="tm-card-toggle"
                      IsCheckedChanged="OnTopMostChanged" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE7E8;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="AutoStartTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="AutoStartSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <ToggleSwitch Grid.Column="2" x:Name="AutoStartToggle" Classes="tm-card-toggle"
                      IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoStartChanged" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE840;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="PinTaskbarTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <!-- The pin state IS this row's description — "not pinned yet", "already pinned", "pinning
               needs a manual step" — so it is the subtitle, not a value line and not a below-row block.
               The code-behind rewrites it from one of eight AppLanguage strings — five in
               ApplyTaskbarPinState's switch, three more in the click handlers. None of the eight
               contains a hard "\n", which is load-bearing rather than incidental: a
               tm-card-sub renders an explicit break as a second line at EVERY window width, so one would
               put this row's header at three lines and move its icon and its button off the y invariant
               I1 centres every other row on. PinToTaskbarFailed carried one until this was written and
               carries the comment saying why it may not; SettingsRowTests guards all eight.
               Long-but-unbroken wording still wraps when the window narrows, the same as every other
               row's subtitle, which is what tm-card-sub's Wrap is for. -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="PinTaskbarStatusText" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <!-- The only button on this row. Its Theme is now STATIC, and Round 7, Task 3 is what made it
             so: TmAccentButton was deleted with the arrival of the Aero control themes (Windows 7 had
             no accent-filled default button), and the run-time Theme swap in ApplyTaskbarPinState went
             with it, because both of its branches had become TmButton.

             What the swap existed for still holds, and is still delivered — by IsEnabled alone. Round
             3, Task 3 (user item 8): once pinned there is no affirmative action left to offer, only a
             completed state to report, and a disabled TmAccentButton still painted a dimmed BLUE
             swatch (its ^:disabled rule only lowers Opacity; it never repainted Background off
             AccentDefaultBrush), which read as a live call to action, just quieter. A disabled
             TmButton has no accent fill to dim in the first place, so "done" now falls out of
             IsEnabled="false" — set by ApplyTaskbarPinState from the live COM pin query — instead of
             out of a second theme. One fewer moving part for the same rendering.

             The static Theme is also what makes the first frame of a process defensible. SettingsPage's
             constructor calls ReloadFromConfig (hence ApplyTaskbarPinState) while this card tree is
             still detached from the logical tree, where FindResource cannot reach Application.Styles at
             all; a Button with no Theme and no Classes would have rendered as stock Fluent for that
             frame. It renders as TmButton instead, which is what it renders as afterwards too. -->
        <Button Grid.Column="2" x:Name="PinTaskbarBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}"
                Click="OnPinTaskbarClick" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE765;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyEnabledTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyEnabledSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <ToggleSwitch Grid.Column="2" x:Name="HotkeyEnabledToggle" Classes="tm-card-toggle"
                      IsCheckedChanged="OnHotkeyEnabledChanged" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <Border Classes="tm-card">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE92E;" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
          <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
          <!-- The help line tells the user how to work the box beside it, so it is this row's
               subtitle. Moving it out from under the Grid moved the box it points at from above the
               sentence to beside it, so AppLanguage's HotkeyHelp was reworded from 上方/"above" to
               右侧/"on the right" in the same commit — the same correction PinToTaskbarNotAllowed and
               PinToTaskbarUnsupported needed for their own button. -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyHelpText" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
        </StackPanel>
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Classes="tm-card-actions">
          <!-- Key capture is wired in code with a TUNNELING KeyDown handler (see the ctor): a
               bubbling handler would run after the TextBox has already consumed the key.

               tm-keycap is what makes this box part of the app rather than stock Fluent. It was the one
               control on the whole 设置 page with no Classes and no Theme, so its disabled fill came
               from Avalonia's own TextControlBackgroundDisabled and measured (201,201,201) against the
               (253,253,253) of every disabled button around it. The class carries the box's size and
               padding too, so nothing here restates them. -->
          <TextBox x:Name="HotkeyBox" Classes="tm-keycap" IsReadOnly="True" />
          <!-- Clearing a hotkey is a reset, not a forward-moving action, so this button never
               qualified for the accent treatment even though it is the row's only button. The
               criterion is moot now: Round 7, Task 3 deleted TmAccentButton along with the rest of
               the accent-filled default button (Windows 7 had none), leaving TmButton the only
               settings-card button theme there is. Kept because it records WHY this row never
               changed when the two buttons that did carry the accent were moved. -->
          <Button x:Name="HotkeyClearBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}"
                  Click="OnHotkeyClearClick" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
